when I was a kid I could find Wonder  pretty much anywhere I looked I could  just turn over a stone in the garden and  be amazed by everything that was  underneath it back then you'd run and  tell all your friends if you found a  ladybug or a four-leaf clover hidden in  the grass and when you saw the first  Firefly of the summer it was a real  event  but over time I guess I've just seen a  lot of ladybugs in my backyard and it  seems like I traveled farther and  farther away to find that feeling of  wonder sometimes it just feels like  there isn't all that much wonder left  here at home but maybe that's the  challenge to find wonder right where you  are about 15 miles from me in watts Los  Angeles there's a tower that looks like  a madman's jungle gym in the middle of a  residential neighborhood in the 1920s an  Italian immigrant named Simon Rodia  built it as his home he built the whole  thing by himself using rebar and coat  hangers and cement local kids would  bring him bottle caps and seashells and  broken bits of pottery that he used to  decorate it that's one way to do it just  build wonder right where you are maybe  as you get older the stones you have to  overturn or less about finding something  new and more about revealing the history  of the thing hidden away in downtown Los  Angeles you can still ride on something  called the angels flight in 1901 it was  the shortest train ride in the world  which is a strange record to hold you  paid one penny in your road for one  minute but even some of the more  spectacular things get richer with a bit  of history up above the city the  Griffith Observatory is pretty awesome  by itself come on it's got a giant  telescope in it what's not to love but  it's also where the Apollo astronauts  learned how to navigate the Stars  imagine that looking up and trying to  memorize where everything was because  you're about to go there they still have  the chair that those astronauts sat in  maybe that's just it  trying to find a chair to experience a  place through someone else's eyes so  what about where you live what are the  stories of wonder in your hometown that  you still have to discover it might take  a little effort but it's amazing when  you find it when you find wonder again
 

